          "text": "     1→---\n     2→title = \"Packaging Caffeine\"\n     3→date = \"2025-11-18\"\n     4→summary = \"Description of our initial build process, based on wonderful work of others.\"\n     5→---\n     6→\n     7→[Gleam](https://gleam.run/) is: _a friendly language for building type-safe systems that scale [which leverages] [t]he power of a type system, the expressiveness of functional programming, and the reliability of the highly concurrent, fault tolerant Erlang runtime, with a familiar and modern syntax._\n     8→\n     9→As Gleam [targets either Erlang or Javascript](https://gleam.run/writing-gleam/gleam-toml/) configured within the gleam.toml, unlike a Rust or a Go, it doesn't compile to a binary out of the box.\n    10→While the `gleam` command line tool itself is pretty _kickass_, coming with a lot of built in ease (i.e. installing deps on `gleam run`), the lack of compilation to a binary makes distribution of a\n    11→compiler a bit of a challenge; I would love to just create a release on my website with versioned binaries for the release.\n    12→\n    13→As I am creating a [Github Action in parallel](https://github.com/Brickell-Research/caffeine_lang_github_action) to server development that I am leveraging at my day job at [Spring Health](https://www.springhealth.com/),\n    14→I found myself searching for an answer to this issue early on. \n    15→\n    16→## The First Naive Solution\n    17→\n    18→As I have setup my Github Action (first time GHA developer... no clue if this is _the_ approach or just where I landed), I have a Dockerfile with an `entrypoint.sh` script.\n    19→\n    20→A condensed version of my Dockerfile is:\n    21→\n    22→```Dockerfile\n    23→FROM alpine:3.18\n    24→\n    25→# Install minimal dependencies\n    26→...\n    27→\n    28→# Install rebar3 manually\n    29→...\n    30→\n    31→# Install Gleam\n    32→...\n    33→\n    34→# Create Gleam project and set up custom main\n    35→WORKDIR /caffeine\n    36→RUN gleam new . \\\n    37→    && gleam add argv \\\n    38→    && gleam add caffeine_lang \\\n    39→    && gleam deps download\n    40→\n    41→# Copy custom main.gleam\n    42→COPY main.gleam src/caffeine.gleam\n    43→\n    44→# Build the project\n    45→RUN gleam build\n    46→\n    47→# Set the entrypoint\n    48→ENTRYPOINT [\"/entrypoint.sh\"]\n    49→```\n    50→\n    51→Where the `entrypoint.sh` is a bash script to consume the required compiler input parameters and feed them to `caffeine` and `main.gleam` is a copy-pasta version of the main file from `caffeine` itself.\n    52→\n    53→*All together:*\n    54→\n    55→1. package `caffeine` and publish package ([see here](https://packages.gleam.run/?search=caffeine))\n    56→\n    57→2. in Dockerfile of GHA, create a new Gleam project where `caffeine` is a dependency\n    58→\n    59→3. move a copy of the command line interface (`main.gleam`) in to the Dockerfile build\n    60→\n    61→4. execute `caffeine` via the Gleam project, passing in the parameters captured\n    62→\n    63→This is exceptionally hacky, but it works 🤷‍♂️. \n    64→\n    65→\n    66→***\n    67→\n    68→## A Slightly Better Solution\n    69→\n    70→Across the web, there are glimmers of hope 🙏\n    71→\n    72→- Yoshie's Lightning talk: _Building self-contained executables with Gleam - Yoshie Reusch | Code BEAM Europe 2025_ ([video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ojs0YREqX8k))\n    73→- An issue in Gleam's repo hinting that _maybe_ with enough financial support, we'd get something ([link](https://github.com/gleam-lang/gleam/issues/2777))\n    74→- [inoas](https://github.com/inoas) on Github recommended existing solutions ([link](https://github.com/gleam-lang/gleam/issues/2777#issuecomment-2007405330)): (1) `gleam export erlang-shipment`, (2) [burrito](https://github.com/burrito-elixir/burrito), (3) building a small docker image, or (4) targetting Javascript then building that with [deno](https://deno.com/) or [node](https://nodejs.org/en).\n    75→- [gleam-mix](https://github.com/gleam-lang/mix_gleam): _an Elixir archive that teaches Mix how to work with Gleam code and dependencies!_\n    76→\n    77→\n    78→While building a small docker image is feasible, I wanted to aim for a true binary and not require folks to install Erlang, nix, or mix. So, possibly in my\n    79→naivety and desire to checkout deno, I went with inoas's fourth option.\n    80→\n    81→The first step here is to just target `javascript` in the `gleam.toml`.\n    82→\n    83→Pretty easy:\n    84→\n    85→```toml\n    86→name = \"caffeine_lang\"\n    87→...\n    88→\n    89→target = \"javascript\"\n    90→\n    91→[dependencies]\n    92→...\n    93→```\n    94→\n    95→Ok, so `gleam build`... and ... I appear to be using an Erlang yaml parser library 🤦‍♂️\n    96→\n    97→```\n    98→➜  caffeine_lang git:(main) gleam build\n    99→  Compiling caffeine_lang\n   100→error: Unsupported target\n   101→   ┌─ /Users/rob/BrickellResearch/caffeine_lang/src/caffeine_lang/phase_1/parser/utils/general_common.gleam:22:11\n   102→   │\n   103→22 │     glaml.parse_file(file_path)\n   104→   │           ^^^^^^^^^^\n   105→\n   106→This value is not available as it is defined using externals, and there is\n   107→no implementation for the JavaScript target.\n   108→\n   109→Hint: Did you mean to build for a different target?\n   110→```\n   111→\n   112→[glaml](https://hexdocs.pm/glaml/index.html) is my yaml parser of choice and it is in fact just a wrapper around [yamerl](https://hex.pm/packages/yamerl) a _YAML 1.2 and JSON parser in pure Erlang_.\n   113→\n   114→Luckily as far as I could tell, it appeared that was the only Erlang [FFI (foreign function interface)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_function_interface) package.\n   115→\n   116→```toml\n   117→[dependencies]\n   118→gleam_stdlib = \">= 0.63.0 and < 1.0.0\"\n   119→simplifile = \">= 2.3.0 and < 3.0.0\"\n   120→argv = \">= 1.0.2 and < 2.0.0\"\n   121→glaml = \"~> 3.0.0\"\n   122→gleeunit = \">= 1.0.0\"\n   123→caffeine_query_language = \">= 0.0.2\"  # my own library\n   124→```\n   125→\n   126→As I was (a) stubborn about compiling to Javascript and (b) too invested in glaml (_my glaml extensions is another planned blog post and maybe even a future upstream contribution_),\n   127→the path forward was an abstraction layer that enabled both an Erlang and a Javascript FFI.\n   128→\n   129→The first step was the addition of the abstraction layer. As I mentioned in the previous sentence, I'd actually already set this up with my `glaml_extended` library which lives within\n   130→a `deps` directory within `src` (_whether or not this organizational structure makes sense remains to be seen...). Thus, I just needed to add an FFI file there (named `yaml.gleam`) which\n   131→acted as a minimal parser around a `.mjs` and `.erl` yaml parser file respectively. Structurally I ended up with:\n   132→\n   133→```\n   134→└── deps\n   135→    ├── README.md\n   136→    └── glaml_extended\n   137→        ├── README.md\n   138→        ├── extractors.gleam\n   139→        ├── yaml.gleam\n   140→        ├── yaml_ffi.erl\n   141→        └── yaml_ffi.mjs\n   142→```\n   143→\n   144→I will admit I used AI for this, specifically [Claude Code](https://www.claude.com/product/claude-code). It made short work of this and even added unit tests.\n   145→\n   146→From there I then installed the Javascript yaml equivalent package [js-yaml](https://github.com/nodeca/js-yaml).\n   147→\n   148→```json\n   149→{\n   150→  \"name\": \"caffeine_lang\",\n   151→  \"version\": \"0.1.0\",\n   152→  \"type\": \"module\",\n   153→  \"dependencies\": {\n   154→    \"js-yaml\": \"^4.1.0\"\n   155→  }\n   156→}\n   157→```\n   158→\n   159→Then finally I just needed to change the import paths within yaml parsing Gleam files. While I got lucky here that I only had to handle this for a single\n   160→library that was fairly isolated (just a single compiler phase), this really wasn't too painful (_again, thanks Claude_)!\n   161→\n   162→With this, we were now ready to compile to binary with Deno. To do this we:\n   163→\n   164→1. created a `main.mjs` that imports the project and runs the Gleam code\n   165→\n   166→```js\n   167→import { main } from \"./build/dev/javascript/caffeine_lang/caffeine_lang.mjs\";\n   168→\n   169→main();\n   170→```\n   171→\n   172→2. compiled with Deno\n   173→\n   174→```bash\n   175→deno compile --allow-read --allow-write --output caffeine main.mjs\n   176→```\n   177→\n   178→\n   179→*Key flags*\n   180→\n   181→```\n   182→--allow-read / --allow-write - permissions for file I/O (your YAML parsing needs these)\n   183→--output <name> - output binary name\n   184→--target - cross-compile (e.g., x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu)\n   185→```\n   186→\n   187→And low and behold:\n   188→\n   189→```\n   190→➜  caffeine_lang git:(main) ✗ ./caffeine \n   191→Caffeine SLI/SLO compiler\n   192→\n   193→Usage:\n   194→  caffeine compile <specification_directory> <instantiation_directory> <output_directory>\n   195→\n   196→Arguments:\n   197→  specification_directory   Directory containing specification files\n   198→  instantiation_directory   Directory containing instantiation files\n   199→  output_directory          Directory to output compiled files\n   200→\n   201→```\n   202→\n   203→*Note*: the binary size here is pretty big: 73mb and still 29mb zipped!\n   204→\n   205→Just to see if this worked, I did a lazy man distribution, zipping the binary and emailing it between my personal MacBook and work MacBook.\n   206→\n   207→![emailing myself](/images/packaging-caffeine_emailing_myself_caffeine_zip.png)\n   208→\n   209→Unsurprisingly, Apple was not too happy to have me run an arbitrary binary I downloaded from Google Drive.\n   210→\n   211→![Apple stops me](/images/packaging-caffeine_apple_security.png)\n   212→\n   213→However, once I _sudo'd_ and said \"I swear this is a good idea\", we got our binary executing!\n   214→\n   215→![it works](/images/packaging-caffeine_it_works.png)\n   216→\n   217→***\n   218→\n   219→## TLDR How to Compile Gleam Application to a Binary with Deno\n   220→\n   221→1. target Javascript\n   222→\n   223→2. FFI any required packages\n   224→\n   225→3. add a Javascript entrypoint\n   226→\n   227→4. compile with Deno\n   228→\n   229→5. profit 🤘\n   230→\n   231→***\n   232→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n",
